---
// The privacy policy. Written from what the software actually does, and checked against the
// code: what a self-hosted instance sends us (a version string, four counts and its signed
// receipts, only when the owner turns it on), how long the network keeps them, what the hosted
// service would hold, and what the site itself collects.
// It is a draft for a lawyer to review before the hosted service opens, and it says so.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const UPDATED = "22 September 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy · Surfing Dog"
  description="What Surfing Dog knows about you, which is almost nothing if you run the inbox yourself, and what will change when we host it for you."
>
  <section class="wrap wrap-read section is-head" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="h-page" id="privacy-h">What we know about you</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Surfing Dog Inbox runs on your own server or cloud account, so we know almost nothing about you. This page
      lists exactly what we do see.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">Last updated {UPDATED}. Surfing Dog Lda, Portugal. Questions: privacy@surfingdog.ai</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="self-h">
    <div class="wrap wrap-read prose">
      <h2 class="h-section" id="self-h">If you run the inbox yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Your customers' messages, bookings and orders stay in your database. We never receive them, so we can't read
        or recover them. The code is open if you want to check.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things leave your inbox, and only if you turn on <b>Network</b> in Settings. It is off by default.
        First, your inbox registers its domain once, then every hour sends its software version, its runtime, and
        how many bookings, orders, quotes and messages it created in the last 24 hours. Counts only, nothing about
        any person.
      </p>
      <p>
        Second, it sends every receipt it issues. Your inbox signs one when a booking is confirmed or an order is paid.
        It records which business issued it, your reference for the booking or order, what kind of promise it is, and
        when, plus the amount if there is a price. A paid order's receipt also says how it was paid (by card, say) when
        your inbox knows.
      </p>
      <p>
        The customer appears as a code, never a name. The code is an HMAC made with a key only your inbox holds, so
        the network can't turn it back into a person, and the same customer at another business gets a different code.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the customer's agent counter-signs a receipt, that signature and the agent's public key are sent too. An
        agent that keeps its key uses the same one at every business, so the receipts it counter-signed can be linked
        to each other. A receipt never carries names, email or postal addresses, phone numbers, message text, what was
        ordered or payment references.
      </p>
      <p>
        The network keeps the hourly counts for 90 days, each receipt and its counter-signature for 540 days after it
        arrives, and each business's totals (receipts issued, and how many were counter-signed) indefinitely. The
        directory shows the public profile your manifest already publishes, the version and runtime your inbox
        reports, and whether it is answering. For receipts it shows only counts and the date of the latest one, never a
        receipt, an amount or a customer's code.
      </p>
      <p>
        Turn Network off and both stop. What the network already holds is kept for the periods above. Point
        <code>network.url</code> at a directory you run yourself and none of it reaches us.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">If we host it for you</h2>
      <p>
        Hosting is not open yet. When it opens, you will be the controller of the data your customers send you, and we
        will be your processor. We will hold it on our own servers in the European Union and use it only to run the
        service for you. For your own account, your address, your billing and how you use the service, we will be the
        controller.
      </p>
      <p>
        You will be able to export everything as a file, and delete everything, from the software without asking us.
        A written processing agreement will be part of the terms before the first customer is onboarded.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">This website</h2>
      <p>
        No analytics, advertising, tracking pixels or third-party scripts, and no cookie banner, because there is
        nothing to consent to. Our servers keep ordinary web logs, including your IP address and the page you asked
        for, for a short time to keep the site up and stop abuse. Cloudflare sits in front of the site and keeps its own
        logs for the same reason.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you join the waitlist or write to us, we keep your message and email address so we can answer, and use them
        for nothing else. Posts on the requests board are public. The email address you give there is used only to
        tell you when your request ships.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the GDPR you can ask for a copy of what we hold about you, ask us to correct or delete it, object to what
        we do with it, and complain to a supervisory authority. In Portugal that is the
        <a class="link" href="https://www.cnpd.pt/" rel="noopener">CNPD</a>. Write to privacy@surfingdog.ai and we will
        answer within a month.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where we rely on legitimate interest rather than your consent, it is to keep the service running and prevent
        abuse, and you can object to that too.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">The network and reviews</h2>
      <p>
        The directory and the receipts above are live. Reputation, for businesses and for customers, is still being
        built. Before it collects anything, this page will say exactly what it collects, how long it keeps it and who
        can see it. It will use only real transactions. The network itself will decide nothing about anyone; each
        business's own rules will. Identifiers will stay pseudonymous. If any of this changes, this page will say so
        before the feature is switched on.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Changes</h2>
      <p>
        The date at the top shows the last change. The site is an open repository, so every edit to this page is a
        public commit. We will announce material changes on the blog.
      </p>

      <div class="glass card" style="margin-top: var(--sp-6)">
        <h3 class="h-card">Where this stands</h3>
        <p class="card-text">
          We wrote this from what the software does today and checked it against the code. A lawyer has not reviewed
          it yet. That happens before hosting opens to anyone but us, and this note comes off the page once it has.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
